<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Poznań</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Posen, Poznan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Poznań is a city in west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> river, the historical capital of Greater Poland. One of the main strongholds of the early Piast state, it became the seat of Poland's first bishopric in 968, and its cathedral holds the tombs of early rulers. Under Prussian rule from 1793 to 1918, apart from the Napoleonic period, it was the capital of the Grand Duchy and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-posen">Province of Posen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/poznan.jpg" alt="Poznań" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aneta Pawska · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stary_Rynek_w_Poznaniu,_widok_z_drona_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Poznań lies on the River Warta in west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, within the Greater Poland region. Its highest point, at 157 metres, is the summit of Morasko hill in the north, within the Morasko meteorite nature reserve, and its lowest, at 60 metres, lies in the Warta valley. The name probably derives from the Polish verb poznać, 'to meet' or 'to know', marking the area as a meeting place. The earliest references are in the chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg">Thietmar of Merseburg</a>, which mentions a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Poznań in 970 and the city in 1005.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For centuries before the Christianization of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, Poznań was an important cultural and political centre of the Western Polans. A fortified stronghold stood between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> and Cybina rivers on what is now Ostrów Tumski, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> built one of his main gords there. His baptism in 966, seen as a defining moment in the Christianization of the Polish state, may have taken place in Poznań.</p><p>After the baptism construction began of Poznań's cathedral, the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and the city was the main seat of Bishop Jordan, the first missionary bishop sent to Poland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-gniezno">Congress of Gniezno</a> in 1000 established the country's first permanent archbishopric at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, though Poznań kept bishops of its own. The cathedral was the burial place of early Piast monarchs, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a>, Casimir I, Przemysł I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysł II</a>. The Town Hall was rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style after a fire in 1536 and finished in 1560.</p><p>In the later 17th and most of the 18th century wars and occupations devastated the city. Prussian authorities later merged the walled city with its suburbs and took down the old walls in the early 19th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> awarded Posen to the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. From 1939 to 1945 Poznań was the capital of the Reichsgau Wartheland, part of Nazi Germany, and many Polish inhabitants were executed, arrested, expelled or used as forced labour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Poznań is the administrative capital of the Greater Poland Voivodeship and the seat of the oldest Polish diocese. It is home to Adam Mickiewicz University and hosts the Poznań International Fair. Its Old Town, Town Hall, cathedral and the Baroque Fara church are among its main heritage sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Poznań” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1815 the Congress of Vienna took the Duchy of Warsaw from France, gave part of it to Prussia as the Grand Duchy of <b>Posen</b> and made the rest, with Warsaw, a nominally independent Kingdom of Poland ruled by the Russian tsar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Prussia's survival was not assured: the Centre Party's council in the Rhine Province called for a Rhineland-Westphalian republic, 600,000 signed for autonomy in Hanover, and a revolt in <b>Posen</b> aimed at restoring Poland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Interwar Poland was the sixth largest country in Europe in 1938, and Warsaw, Kraków, <b>Poznań</b>, Wilno and Lwów became major European cities with internationally acclaimed universities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When Dąbrowski and Wybicki arrived in <b>Poznań</b> on 6 November 1806, crowds greeted them with the song, and it became one of the most popular patriotic songs of the Duchy of Warsaw.</blockquote><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a></li><li><blockquote>Mieszko I, Duke of Poland, was baptised with much of his court on Holy Saturday, most likely at <b>Poznań</b> or Gniezno.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the testament of Boleslaw III in 1138, his son Mieszko III the Old received Greater Poland, with <b>Poznan</b> and Gniezno, where the Piast state had begun.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Duchy of Greater Poland</a></li></ul></section>
